# Line endings, normalised once so they stop being a source of phantom diffs.
#
# Symptom this fixes: ~200 files under data/ showed as fully rewritten in
# `git status` while `git diff -w` showed nothing at all. Every line had gained
# a CR. On Windows, without this file and without core.autocrlf set, whatever
# tool last wrote those files decided the line ending, and git faithfully
# recorded the churn.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: git decides what is text, stores LF in the index,
# and checks out LF. One canonical form in history, on every machine.
#
# The explicit `binary` lines are not redundant — `text=auto` guesses, and a
# wrong guess on a binary file corrupts it rather than merely annoying you.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF even on Windows, or bash refuses them inside Docker.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.pyd binary
*.safetensors binary
*.bin binary
*.onnx binary
